SICK::Temp (168 lines) is the private temporary directory every key passes through. It becomes a Moo class with Path::Tiny underneath.

What must survive, each pinned by t/22-temp.t:
- the directory is 0700, files in it are created 0600 and exclusively (O_EXCL): a key file never exists with a wider mode, not even for a moment;
- the directory is removed when the object goes out of scope (DESTROY today, DEMOLISH then), and cleanup removes everything this process created, at END and from a signal handler;
- the object stringifies to its path.

Trap to decide and name in the report: SICK::Command::run refuses references in its argument list (grep { !defined || ref } @$argv), so a Path::Tiny object handed to openssl or step as an argument croaks today. Either path and file return strings, or Command::run learns to accept objects that stringify. Callers are SICK::Bundle, both issuers, both targets and ../sick-target-ciscosb.

Done when: Moo class, Path::Tiny for the paths, the three properties above still tested and green, cpanfile updated, prove -lr t green with skips named, and the Cisco suite still passes:
( cd ../sick-target-ciscosb && PERL5LIB=$OLDPWD/lib:$PERL5LIB prove -lr t )

Temp is Moo on Path::Tiny (_dir InstanceOf Path::Tiny, _pid Int, DEMOLISH). Trap decided: path/file keep returning plain strings; Command::run stays strict. Key files still sysopen O_EXCL with mode (Path::Tiny has no exclusive create with a mode). cpanfile/Dockerfile/README/SICK.pm/Changes name Path::Tiny. prove -lr t and Cisco suite green; e2e not run.

